#{[{:activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186193457} :activity/id #uuid "2acc7e09-91cc-5ff3-8a20-8051f891cc61" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050190 :topic/id #uuid "35070aa4-19de-5bfc-b9cc-08a2e69ce882" :topic/math-academy-id 4026} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186193457 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186193941 :tutorial/content "Suppose we want to solve the following system of linear equations using the elimination method:\n\n$$\n{x + 2y = 1; x - y =-2\n$$\n\nAs it stands, adding the two equations will not eliminate any of the variables.\n\nHowever, suppose we want to eliminate the variable $x$. We begin by multiplying the second equation by $\\color{blue}-1$, which gives the following equivalent system:\n\n$$\n{(x + 2y = 1; (x - y =-2)\\cdot (-1)\n$$\n\nThen, we add the two resulting equations together. This will eliminate the variable $x$, allowing us to solve for $y$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nx + 2y &= 1 \\\\\n- x + y &= 2 \\\\\n(2y + y) &= (1 + 2) \\\\\n3y &= 3 \\\\\ny &= 1\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the solution must have $y=1$. However, we still need to find $x$. To do this, we substitute $y=1$ back into one of the equations. Let's choose the second equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nx - y &= -2 \\\\\nx - (1) &= -2 \\\\\nx &= -1\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution to the system of equations is $x=-1$ and $y=1$, which can be expressed as $(-1, 1)$." :tutorial/id #uuid "97211668-1a0d-5471-9a0a-c223dfb2f8a6" :tutorial/math-academy-id 8703 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/id #uuid "6912b472-9f6a-508c-a69c-38d65838fd66" :step/math-academy-id 196622 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186193458}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186193458 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186192654 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186192585 :question/id #uuid "c700cc22-203a-5ad3-913c-e5eb507e22e3" :question/math-academy-id "e-13995"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "93dcc73c-ce2d-5fa3-b32b-9fc600fb269e" :knowledge-point/key-prereq